  "dimensions": "68.5 × 44.5 cm",
  "production_date": "about 1480 - 1500",
  "brief_description": "The Virgin Mary gently supports the Christ Child as he plays with her hair. He casts a curious glance at his cousin, Saint John the Baptist, who is recognisable by the fine wooden cross tucked under his arm, a symbol of Christ's crucifixion. The Virgin's fair complexion and golden hair were considered ideals of female beauty in fifteenth-century Florence, where appearance and virtue were closely linked; as such, she is also meant to be seen as the height of virtue, and an example to all women.\n\nThis gentle image of maternal love was well-suited to worship in the home. Images like this were hugely popular, and Perugino and members of his workshop -- who worked to his designs and in his style -- would have produced numerous similar pictures to meet the demand. This one was probably painted by one of his close associates.",
